          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"The rose\",\"addressee\":\"The little prince\",\"anchor_quote\":\"je t'aime\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression is a direct avowal of the rose's loving emotional state. The following admission that he had not known it because of her fault presents the love as a previously concealed or poorly communicated truth.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The occurrence does not create, renew, or invoke an obligation binding the rose to the little prince. In the same turn, she urges him to be happy and relinquishes his protective care by rejecting the globe. Those statements make the avowal a farewell disclosure rather than an undertaking. Her instructions and release are contextual speech acts, not promises conveyed by the focal words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu as décidé de partir. Va-t'en.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The avowal is represented as a controlled, explanatory disclosure, not a reflex-like cry or affective discharge. The surrounding description emphasizes calmness, while her crying is withheld from the addressee rather than discharged through the focal expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Car elle ne voulait pas qu'il la vît pleurer.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"No core force beyond the truth-presenting avowal is established for the exact expression. Its corrective and revelatory role in the farewell is adequately captured by T.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"At the little prince's departure, after apologizing for her earlier foolishness, the rose calmly tells him that she loves him and explains that he failed to know this through her fault. She then wishes him happiness, dismisses his protective concern, and tells him to leave before he sees her cry.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal avowal does not establish a promise or relational obligation. The rose separately relinquishes the little prince's caretaking role and accepts his decision to depart. No uptake of an undertaking occurs; he is depicted as surprised and confused by her calm tenderness, but he gives no response to the avowal in the supplied scene.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognizable as Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly supports the represented sincerity of the rose's affection through the narrator's references to her tenderness and concealed tears, but it cannot independently establish every aspect of her inner state. The assessment is limited to the supplied passages and does not rely on omitted parts of the work.\"}"
